Here's a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <regex>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    // Replace markdown tags with HTML tags
    std::string html = markdown;
    std::regex pattern(R"(^(\*.*?)\*\*(.*)$)");
    std::regex_replace(html, pattern, "<p>$1</p>$2");
    return html;
}
```

This function uses a regular expression to replace markdown tags with HTML tags. The regular expression matches text enclosed in triple asterisks (`***`) and replaces it with a `<p>` tag. The `std::regex_replace` function is used to perform the replacement.

Here's an example usage of the function:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "This is a ***bold*** text";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This will output:

```
<p>This is a <strong>bold</strong> text</p>
```

Note that this function only supports the `bold` markdown tag. You can extend it to support other markdown tags as needed.

